\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}
Effective parameter-based self-evolution in task streams requires three
complementary capabilities: Experience Accumulation, Experience Transfer,
and Experience Interference Suppression.
Accordingly, we introduce \METHODNAME{}, a framework that accumulates
successful interaction experience, reuses it to guide exploration, and
selectively internalizes it into the policy.
Our central distinction is between \emph{successful behavior} and
\emph{transferable guidance}: an environment-verified trajectory provides
evidence of task success, but neither its hindsight explanation nor its
applicability to another task is automatically validated.

Experience Accumulation combines experience-guided policy learning with
local hindsight distillation (Sec.~\ref{sec:exp_accu}).
Experience Transfer abstracts task-specific skills into reusable guidance
and grounds their cross-task use in new environment feedback
(Sec.~\ref{sec:exp_transfer}).
Experience Interference Suppression restricts hindsight distillation to
successful trajectories whose skill contexts provide additional evidence
for the observed behavior (Sec.~\ref{sec:interference}).


\subsection{Experience Accumulation}
\label{sec:exp_accu}

At evolution step $k$, the current policy $\pi_{\theta_k}$ receives a batch
of tasks from the stream.
For each task $q$, it collects a main-pass group $\mathcal{G}_q$ by
sampling actions $a_i \sim \pi_{\theta_k}(\cdot \mid h_i)$, where $h_i$
contains the task, observations, and interaction history.
A completed trajectory $\tau$ comprises these interactions and terminal
feedback $\mathcal{F}_\tau$; $S(\tau)\in\{0,1\}$ indicates verified
success.
The pool $\mathcal{P}_k$ contains global experience accumulated before
this evolution step.


\paragraph{Hierarchical Experience Learning.}
\METHODNAME{} uses experience at two complementary scopes.
\emph{Local experience} consists of successful trajectories within the
current task and their hindsight skills.
For a successful trajectory, the current policy extracts
\begin{equation}
    z_\tau^{\mathrm{local}}
    =
    \operatorname{Analyze}_{\theta_k}(\tau,\mathcal{F}_\tau),
\end{equation}
which summarizes decision rules and effective procedures in that
interaction.
Following SEED~\citep{wu2026seedselfevolvingonpolicydistillation}, a
one-time skill-alignment initialization equips the policy with this
analysis capability (Appendix~\ref{sec:implementation}).
\emph{Global experience} consists of abstracted versions of successful
local skills retained for reuse across tasks.
The hierarchy concerns the scope of experience, rather than separate
teacher models: a local skill may also contribute an abstracted version
to the global pool.

Experience enters learning through two mechanisms.
First, a trajectory's own local skill provides a hindsight context for
re-scoring its sampled actions and constructing a filtered OPD objective
(Sec.~\ref{sec:interference}).
Second, successful experience guides \emph{new} interactions.
For a main-pass group containing both successes and failures, we use its
shortest successful sibling trajectory as a reference for re-running the
same task.
For an all-failure group, we instead attempt to retrieve a global skill
from $\mathcal{P}_k$ (Sec.~\ref{sec:exp_transfer}).
Let $c_q$ denote either reference, and let $H(h,c)$ augment history $h$
with experience $c$.
The additional trajectories are sampled as
\begin{equation}
    \widetilde{\tau}
    \sim
    \operatorname{Rollout}(\pi_{\theta_k},q;c_q),
    \qquad
    \widetilde{a}_i
    \sim
    \pi_{\theta_k}(\cdot\mid H(\widetilde{h}_i,c_q)).
\end{equation}
No additional pass is made for all-success groups or when no eligible
reference is available.
When training on the new trajectories, we remove the extra reference
while preserving their actual interaction histories.
This transfers experience-conditioned behavior into the ordinary policy
through outcome-driven learning, rather than treating the reference
itself as a correct action target.


\paragraph{Joint Training Objective.}
The training batch combines the main pass with the two sources of
experience-guided resampling:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{D}_k
    =
    \mathcal{D}_k^{\mathrm{main}}
    \cup
    \mathcal{D}_k^{\mathrm{sibling}}
    \cup
    \mathcal{D}_k^{\mathrm{pool}}.
\end{equation}
Main-pass trajectories use ordinary group-relative reward advantages.
For resampled trajectories, we instead use a \emph{source-group baseline}:
each decision's reward is centered against the main-pass statistics of
the task that generated it, not the statistics of its new resampling
group.
The normalization scale is also computed from main-pass rewards;
details are provided in Appendix~\ref{sec:implementation}.
Consequently, an all-success resampling group can retain positive
advantages relative to a mixed or all-failure source group, rather than
losing the improvement signal through within-group centering.

We optimize a clipped group-relative policy-gradient objective
$\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PG}}$ on $\mathcal{D}_k$.
Both old and updated policy probabilities are evaluated under ordinary
contexts, including for trajectories sampled with additional experience;
this is reference-guided data collection, not strictly on-policy sampling
from the ordinary-context policy for the additional pass.
The joint objective is
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{evo}}
    =
    \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PG}}
    +
    \lambda_{\mathrm{OPD}}
    \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}}^{\mathrm{filtered}}
    +
    \lambda_{\mathrm{KL}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}},
    \label{eq:evolution_objective}
\end{equation}
where the filtered local OPD term is defined in
Sec.~\ref{sec:interference}, and $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}$ is standard
regularization against the fixed initialization policy.
Global skills affect learning through resampled behavior, not a separate
global OPD loss.
Successful trajectories from either pass can produce local skills for
distillation and pool curation.
After the update, $\pi_{\theta_{k+1}}$ acts and analyzes subsequent
interactions, while newly admitted skills become available in
$\mathcal{P}_{k+1}$.
Thus, experience accumulates both in explicit memory and in policy
parameters; the following section specifies which experience is retained
for cross-task reuse.


\subsection{Experience Transfer}
\label{sec:exp_transfer}

Success on one task does not establish that its experience applies to
another.
\METHODNAME{} therefore separates the construction of potentially
transferable guidance from the environment feedback used to learn from
its application.


\paragraph{Boundary-Aware Experience Curation.}
We use \emph{boundary} to refer to the distinction between
instance-specific details and reusable decision rules, rather than an
observed task-switch boundary.
A successful local skill may mix a useful procedure with names, object
identifiers, or values that are valid only in its originating task.
Transferring the whole description risks carrying these incidental
details into a different interaction.

For each candidate skill extracted from a successful trajectory, the
current policy assesses the raw skill's transferability and constructs a
\emph{de-instantiated} version:
\begin{equation}
    (v_\tau,g_\tau)
    =
    \operatorname{JudgeRewrite}_{\theta_k}
    (z_\tau^{\mathrm{local}}).
\end{equation}
Here $v_\tau\in[0,1]$ scores the candidate's reuse potential, while
$g_\tau$ is instructed to retain decision rules and action order but
remove instance-specific entities, values, and item lists.
Candidates are accepted for storage when $v_\tau\geq\eta$, where $\eta$
is the admission threshold.
The bounded pool stores the resulting skill text, a frozen text-encoder
embedding, and its task provenance.
Abstracting a global version does not replace the original local skill
used for hindsight distillation.
This procedure controls the scope of retained experience through
abstraction and screening; it does not assume an explicitly known
applicability region for each skill.
Pool maintenance and admission budgets are detailed in
Appendix~\ref{sec:implementation}.


\paragraph{Outcome-Grounded Cross-Task Transfer.}
When all trajectories in $\mathcal{G}_q$ fail, no successful sibling is
available to guide further exploration.
We form a retrieval query $x_q$ from the task description and initial
observation, and select
\begin{equation}
    g_q^\star
    =
    \operatorname*{arg\,max}_{\substack{g\in\mathcal{P}_k\\
                                      \operatorname{src}(g)\neq q}}
    \operatorname{sim}\!\left(E(x_q),E(g)\right),
\end{equation}
where $E$ is a frozen text encoder, $\operatorname{sim}$ is cosine
similarity, and $\operatorname{src}(g)$ identifies the originating task.
We use $g_q^\star$ only if its similarity meets a relevance threshold;
an empty candidate set or a below-threshold match yields no pool-guided
resampling.
This excludes same-task reuse without imposing hard domain partitions.

The retrieved skill supplies $c_q$ for the resampling procedure in
Sec.~\ref{sec:exp_accu}.
Unlike a same-task successful trajectory, it provides no direct evidence
that its suggested procedure succeeds on $q$.
We therefore treat retrieval as a proposal for exploration rather than a
distillation target: the environment evaluates the newly generated
behavior, and source-referenced rewards determine its policy-gradient
signal.
In particular, rescuing an all-failure source group yields positive
reward differences relative to its unsuccessful main pass.
The skill is removed from the training context, so these outcomes train
the ordinary policy rather than a policy dependent on retrieved prompts.
This grounds cross-task transfer in observed behavior, while the next
section addresses a separate risk: reinforcing an uninformative
hindsight explanation of behavior that has already succeeded.



\subsection{Experience Interference Suppression}
\label{sec:interference}

Experience can interfere with learning when hindsight supervision
reinforces unsuccessful behavior or adds imitation pressure without
informative guidance.
We address these two sources of interference in the local OPD term;
the outcome-based updates on main-pass and resampled trajectories remain
active independently of these filters.

\paragraph{Success-Conditioned Distillation.}
For trajectory $\tau$, let $\mathcal{T}_\tau$ index its valid response
tokens, with token $y_{\tau,j}$ and ordinary context $h_{\tau,j}$,
including the preceding response tokens.
For trajectories with a local skill, define the teacher and student
log-probabilities
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
        u_{\tau,j}
        &= \log\pi_{\theta_k}
        (y_{\tau,j}\mid H(h_{\tau,j},z_\tau^{\mathrm{local}})),\\
        s_{\tau,j}(\theta)
        &= \log\pi_{\theta}(y_{\tau,j}\mid h_{\tau,j}).
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Following hindsight OPD, a token receives loss
$\ell_{\tau,j}=w_{\tau,j}(u_{\tau,j}-s_{\tau,j}(\theta))$, with
\begin{equation}
    w_{\tau,j}
    =
    \operatorname{sg}\!\left[
        \sigma\!\left(\beta
        (u_{\tau,j}-s_{\tau,j}(\theta))\right)
    \right],
\end{equation}
where $\sigma$ is the sigmoid, $\beta$ controls its sharpness, and
$\operatorname{sg}$ stops gradients.
Teacher log-probabilities are fixed during the update.
Since $\partial\ell_{\tau,j}/\partial s_{\tau,j}=-w_{\tau,j}$, this
term increases the probability of sampled tokens even when the teacher
gap is negative.
On failed trajectories, that pressure can oppose reward-driven learning.
We therefore analyze and distill only verified successful trajectories.
Let $\mathcal{V}_k\subseteq\mathcal{D}_k$ contain those successful
trajectories for which a valid local skill is obtained.
Failures remain in $\mathcal{D}_k$ for policy-gradient learning, but
receive no OPD supervision.


\paragraph{Trajectory-Level Evidence Gating.}
A successful outcome validates the behavior, not necessarily its
hindsight explanation.
We retain a skill's distillation signal only when its context increases
the mean log-likelihood of the successful trajectory:
\begin{equation}
    \Delta_\tau
    =
    \frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}_\tau|}
    \sum_{j\in\mathcal{T}_\tau}
    \left[u_{\tau,j}-s_{\tau,j}(\theta_k)\right],
    \qquad
    m_\tau=\mathbf{1}[\Delta_\tau>0].
\end{equation}
The gate is computed before optimization using the same policy weights
under both contexts, and applies to the trajectory as a whole rather
than selecting isolated positive-gap tokens.
It is an evidence criterion for hindsight supervision, not a guarantee
of skill correctness or transferability.
The filtered term in Eq.~\ref{eq:evolution_objective} is
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{OPD}}^{\mathrm{filtered}}
    =
    \frac{1}{N_k}
    \sum_{\tau\in\mathcal{V}_k}
    m_\tau
    \sum_{j\in\mathcal{T}_\tau}
    w_{\tau,j}\left[u_{\tau,j}-s_{\tau,j}(\theta)\right],
\end{equation}
where $N_k=\sum_{\tau\in\mathcal{D}_k}|\mathcal{T}_\tau|$ counts all
valid response tokens, not only those retained for distillation.
This normalization avoids amplifying the remaining supervision as the
filters reduce its coverage.
Together, success conditioning and trajectory-level gating restrict
hindsight reinforcement to verified behavior with a positive contextual
likelihood gain, while experience-guided exploration continues to learn
from environment outcomes.


